============================================================
INTERFACE SEGREGATION PRINCIPLE (ISP) – THEORY POINTS
============================================================

1️⃣ Definition
--------------
Interface Segregation Principle states:
“Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use.”

In simple words:
→ A class should implement ONLY those methods that are relevant to it.


2️⃣ Core Idea
-------------
• Avoid large, “fat” interfaces
• Prefer multiple small, specific interfaces
• Each interface should represent ONE role or responsibility


3️⃣ Why ISP is Needed
---------------------
• Prevents unnecessary method implementations
• Avoids dummy or empty method bodies
• Reduces confusion in code
• Makes system easier to maintain and extend
• Improves readability and clarity of responsibilities


4️⃣ What Happens If ISP Is Violated
----------------------------------
• Classes are forced to implement methods they don’t need
• Leads to meaningless implementations like:
  - "Not Supported"
  - Empty methods
• Changes in one method may affect unrelated classes
• Code becomes fragile and hard to refactor


5️⃣ Benefits of Following ISP
-----------------------------
✔ Cleaner and more readable code
✔ Clear separation of responsibilities
✔ Easier testing (mock only required interfaces)
✔ Better scalability
✔ Aligns closely with real-world roles


6️⃣ Real-World Analogy
---------------------
In an office:
• Developer → writes code
• Designer → designs UI
• Admin → repairs coffee machine

You should NOT force:
• Developer to repair coffee machine
• Admin to write code

Each role has its own interface.


7️⃣ ISP vs Fat Interface
-----------------------
Fat Interface:
• Many unrelated methods
• One interface for everything
• Forces unwanted implementations

ISP-Based Design:
• Multiple small interfaces
• Each interface has a single purpose
• Classes implement only what they need


8️⃣ ISP in Android Development
------------------------------
Common Android examples:
• OnClickListener
• OnLongClickListener
• TextWatcher (beforeTextChanged, onTextChanged, afterTextChanged)
• ViewModel vs View responsibilities
• Repository interfaces with small contracts


9️⃣ Relation to SOLID
--------------------
ISP is the "I" in SOLID:
• S – Single Responsibility Principle
• O – Open Closed Principle
• L – Liskov Substitution Principle
• I – Interface Segregation Principle
• D – Dependency Inversion Principle


🔟 Interview One-Liner
---------------------
“Interface Segregation Principle ensures that classes are not forced to implement methods they do not use by splitting large interfaces into smaller, focused ones.”
